{"qid":"Q600524","names":["Sokoto Caliphate"],"title":"Sokoto Caliphate","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Sokoto Caliphate was a Sunni Islamic state in the western Sudan, created through the jihad led by the Fulani scholar Usman dan Fodio beginning in 1804; by 1808, when the Hausa state of Gobir fell, the core territories were secured and reorganized into some thirty emirates under dan Fodio's sons and disciples. At its peak the caliphate was the largest state in sub-Saharan Africa, encompassing most of present-day northern Nigeria, southern Niger, and parts of Cameroon, with Sokoto as the spiritual capital and Gwandu controlling the western emirates. British forces under Frederick Lugard captured Sokoto in March 1903, killing the reigning caliph Attahiru I at the battle of Burmi; the territory was incorporated into the Protectorate of Northern Nigeria, though Sokoto's religious authority persisted under indirect rule.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The caliphate's emirate system left administrative records that allow reasonably confident mapping of core territories; peripheral zones of tribute and raiding beyond the main emiral centers are conjectural, and boundaries with neighboring states like Bornu are approximated.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Usman dan Fodio","url":"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Usman-dan-Fodio"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Sokoto Caliphate","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sokoto_Caliphate"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Sokoto Caliphate","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Usman dan Fodio (Usman ibn Fudi)","nativeName":"عثمان بن فودي","roman":"ʿU